--- What this verifies ----------------------------------------------------
1. The vanilla path on dualopend.c:4340 (`tal(NULL, struct state)`) returns
   uninitialised memory under glibc's tal pool reuse, exactly as the
   AFL+Nyx campaign hit non-deterministically.
2. The read at dualopend.c:1283 (`if (!state->reconnected)`) traps under
   UBSan when the byte is non-bool — the canonical "load of value 254"
   error signature reproduced here matches the patch's commit message.
3. The patched path (`talz` instead of `tal`) zero-fills the entire
   allocation, so the same read site reads 0 and exits cleanly.

The poison covers the whole allocation (memset 0xFE), so the demonstration
is robust to the exact offset of `reconnected` — what is being verified is
the allocator's behaviour (tal returns uninit; talz zero-fills) on a
real-shaped struct, not field-offset arithmetic.
